CBS News chief congressional  correspondent Nancy Cordes and CBS news  political correspondent Edie O'Keeffe  join us to discuss all this welcome to  both of you all right  Edie let's take a look at some of those  exit polls about why there was such  massive turnout we learned that  two-thirds of those who voted said they  were voting to send a message to Trump  1/4 of voters casted in support of the  president 4 in 10 cast votes to oppose  him 1/3 said it had nothing to do with  him but that signals that the president  much as he wanted it to be much as we  expected it would be it became about him  what about the number of moderate  Republicans who lost their seats in the  House a dying breed at this point we're  down to basically we'll heard of Texas I  know we'll be here later this morning  guys like Carlos corbello in the Miami  area lost mike coffman in the Denver  suburbs those have been longtime  Democratic targets and in a year like  this they lost and those are the  moderates who didn't decide to retire  because they took a look at the  environment and saw that it was  inhospitable to Republicans in swing  districts and Nancy you cover the Senate  of course Democrats lost a number of  seats Republicans enlarged and their  majority right and Democrats frankly  were stunned they thought perhaps they  might lose the Heidi Heitkamp race in  North Dakota or Claire McCaskill in  Missouri they were not expecting that  Joe Donnelly in Indiana would lose and  now Senator Bill Nelson of Florida also  on the cusp of losing I was talking to  Senator Chris Coons of Delaware who was  not up for re-election but is now going  to have to say goodbye to a number of  his colleagues he said he's heartbroken  and he's worried about what this means  for the party because you have so many  of the moderate deal makers in the  Senate Democratic caucus leaving meaning  the Democrats who were living in  Republican states correct and they have  to cut deals to survive right what does  this mean if anything for Chuck Schumer  the Democratic Party now you know they  lost some of their races was the  Democrats think well you know  structurally it was just a bad year for  us or are they going to look for new  leaders right they are going to want to  avoid drawing any lessons from this race  because they're gonna say well look it  was just the map we were up in all of  these red states you know what did you  think was going to happen but there are  going to be some Democrats making the  case first of all we got to figure out  what's going on in Florida this is  supposed to be a swing state and  yet even in a year where everything  should have been going the Democrats way  it didn't well just a follow on John  what about all of those Democrats who  lost in red states in the Senate all  voted against Kavanagh the exit polls  showed that was a factor now whether it  was the factor whether it was a  contributing factor I think we're gonna  be sorting that out for several weeks to  come  Republicans would like you to think this  morning that it had something to do with  it and certainly we know that it helped  drive Republican interest in energy and  turnout in the closing weeks to draw a  correlation right but you know again man  these were states that the president had  won by big numbers they were already  gonna be on you know they were gonna be  big targets anyway and look at Joe  Manchin who won right you know did fall  and he voted for him that's someone like  Phil Bredesen  in Tennessee who was a potential pickup  for for the Democratic Party very  popular in Tennessee even though he's a  Democrat he said that he would have  voted for Cavanaugh and he lost Isis or  so yeah give us your sense then what  would have happened in those cases in a  Democratic Institute to where women are  very important to turning out your vote  if those senators had said I'm gonna  vote for Cavanaugh I mean there would  have been a downside on the enero which  was the calculation for Joe Donnelly and  for Claire McCaskill especially because  they understood that they needed to  drive up support that they did have  their and hope that there were enough  independents elsewhere they didn't have  them by the way if you think this was  topsy-turvy remember that in 2020  Republicans will have the problem that  Democrats that this year were seats to  defend in blue states there was one  moment of unity last night 8 and 10  voters said they want more women in  power in Congress holding that political  seats and women made moderate gains last  night we're gonna end up at the end of  the day with most likely one women more  woman senator than we had before so  instead of 23 you'd have 24 we saw women  making good great at race as well they  did and and moderate gains in the house  as well they go up a little bit from  their record but still a long way to go  yeah at a Nancy we keep talking for  hours as we did last night but  unfortunately don't have that much time  this morning great if you need us  like cbsn always on

